---
import Case from '../Case.astro';
import Shot from '../Shot.astro';
import Photo from '../Photo.astro';
import Deep from '../Deep.astro';
import home from '../../assets/shots/visiondrive-home.webp';
import mobile from '../../assets/shots/visiondrive-mobile.webp';
import enrol from '../../assets/shots/visiondrive-enrol.webp';

const facts = [
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Sole developer, from design to deploys' },
  { k: 'Result', v: 'A perfect NZTA compliance audit' },
  { k: 'Built with', v: 'ABP Framework on ASP.NET Core, PostgreSQL, Azure Blob Storage' },
  { k: 'Live', v: 'visiondrive.nz', href: 'https://visiondrive.nz' },
];

const route = [
  ['Book', 'Four steps online, or a whole team at once.'],
  ['Verify', 'ID checked against the strongest document the course accepts.'],
  ['Train', 'Sessions from recurring schedules: campus, tutor, capacity.'],
  ['Assess', 'Checklists built from shared sections, then signed off.'],
  ['Certify', 'Certificates issued from tracked blank stock.'],
  ['Report', 'NZQA registers to Excel. Audits keep their evidence.'],
  ['Bill', 'Card payments, invoices and credit notes, posted to the books.'],
];

const credits = ['Design', 'Front end', 'Back end', 'Database', 'Payments and invoicing', 'Compliance reporting', 'Deploys', 'Support'];

const notes = [
  '<strong>Two stacks so far.</strong> It began on ASP.NET, React and MariaDB. I migrated it onto ABP Framework and PostgreSQL, with files in Azure Blob Storage and email through SMTP2Go and MailKit.',
  '<strong>Invoices that match the books.</strong> Invoices and credit notes integrate with Microsoft Dynamics 365 Business Central, carrying the product codes and tax rates it expects. Removing a learner raises a credit note after a short delay, so several removals become one.',
  '<strong>Card payments confirm the booking.</strong> Individuals pay through Stripe Checkout, reconciled from webhooks, so an abandoned checkout leaves no invoice behind. Company and MSD-funded bookings are invoiced instead.',
  '<strong>Schedules instead of data entry.</strong> Sessions are generated from recurring schedules, which staff can pause or close to the public.',
  '<strong>The strongest ID a course accepts.</strong> Document types are ranked, each learner is offered the best one their course takes, and submissions wait in a review queue.',
  '<strong>Compliance as a by-product.</strong> Certificates come from tracked stock, NZQA assessor, trainee and certificate registers export to Excel, and changes land in an audit log.',
  '<strong>Machines have paperwork too.</strong> Every training machine carries its own compliance certificates and service status.',
  '<strong>Where bookings come from.</strong> Referral partners get tracked links, and bookings made after following one are credited to them.',
];
---

<Case
  id="endrive"
  name="EnDrive"
  line="Booking, compliance and records for driver training schools."
  facts={facts}
  t0={8.5}
  t1={12.75}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="ed-media">
      <Shot
        class="ed-desk"
        src={home}
        alt="The Vision Drive home page, offering car, forklift and truck driver training with buttons to book."
        sizes="(min-width: 90rem) 64rem, (min-width: 48rem) 74vw, 100vw"
      />
      <Shot
        class="ed-phone"
        src={mobile}
        alt="The same page on a phone."
        sizes="(min-width: 90rem) 17rem, (min-width: 48rem) 20vw, 40vw"
        widths={[360, 540, 720, 1080]}
        phone
      />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap story">
    <p class="lede">
      EnDrive started as my two-month internship project at Enform in October 2025: a course booking system that had
      to meet NZTA and NZQA requirements.
    </p>
    <div class="story-body">
      <p>
        At the end of it I presented the system to a room of industry people, educators and students. Activate
        Education Group, Enform’s parent company, kept me on to finish it, and I’ve owned it since: the design, the
        conversations with the people who use it, the code and the deploys.
      </p>
      <p>
        It runs Vision Drive, which trains car, forklift and truck drivers in Hamilton. The data entry, moderation and
        reporting features I added helped the school to a perfect NZTA compliance audit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The public site takes the bookings. Behind it, a 24-screen admin follows each learner from booking to
        certificate, and keeps the records a training provider is audited on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure class="wrap stage">
    <div class="stage-photos">
      <Photo
        name="stage-wide"
        class="stage-wide"
        alt="Billy on stage at a lectern, gesturing to the audience as he presents. Behind him the Vision Drive home page, Forklift and Truck Driver Training, fills a large screen."
        sizes="(min-width: 90rem) 52rem, (min-width: 48rem) 62vw, 100vw"
        ratio="3 / 2"
        focus="50% 50%"
      />
      <Photo
        name="stage-close"
        class="stage-close"
        alt="Billy speaking into a microphone in front of his title slide: Billy Hancock, Vision Drive Booking System."
        sizes="(min-width: 90rem) 26rem, (min-width: 48rem) 30vw, 100vw"
        ratio="4 / 5"
        focus="58% 40%"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption class="cap">
      Presenting the first version, then called the Vision Drive Booking System, at the end of my internship.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="route-wrap">
      <h4 class="sub">A learner’s path through EnDrive</h4>
      <ol class="route">
        {
          route.map(([title, text]) => (
            <li>
              <p class="route-step">{title}</p>
              <p class="route-text">{text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap ed-pair">
    <figure class="ed-enrol">
      <Shot
        src={enrol}
        alt="The first step of booking on visiondrive.nz: choosing whether the booking is for yourself or for a team."
        sizes="(min-width: 90rem) 40rem, (min-width: 48rem) 52vw, 175vw"
      />
      <figcaption class="cap">
        Booking starts by asking who it’s for, because a company booking a team is invoiced while an individual pays at
        checkout.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="credits" role="group" aria-labelledby="credits-title">
      <p class="credits-title" id="credits-title">Credits</p>
      <p class="credits-line">Every role, from design to support: Billy Hancock.</p>
      <dl>
        {
          credits.map((role) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{role}</dt>
              <dd>Billy Hancock</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Deep notes={notes} />
  </div>
</Case>
